uperf-linux GUI edition for Debian and Ubuntu
================================================

This package is the complete GUI edition. It contains the same daemon, CLI,
probe, system integration, configuration, and safety behavior as the headless
uperf-linux package, plus the GTK application.

The package deliberately does not enable or start uperf-linux.service.
When /etc/uperf-linux/device.json is absent, the daemon selects one exact
device-tree match from /usr/share/uperf-linux/devices. A retained or manually
created /etc file is an administrator override and takes precedence.

Validate the complete live configuration before enabling the service:

  workdir="$(mktemp -d)"
  profile=/usr/share/uperf-linux/devices/REVIEWED-PROFILE.json
  install -m 0644 "$profile" "$workdir/device.json"
  install -m 0644 /etc/uperf-linux/policy.json "$workdir/policy.json"
  if test -f /var/lib/uperf-linux/apps.json; then
      install -m 0600 /var/lib/uperf-linux/apps.json "$workdir/apps.json"
  else
      install -m 0644 /usr/share/uperf-linux/defaults/apps.json \
          "$workdir/apps.json"
  fi
  uperfctl config validate "$workdir"
  rm -rf "$workdir"

After inspection and successful validation, an administrator may run:

  systemctl enable --now uperf-linux.service

Experimental SCHED_FIFO plans remain blocked by RestrictRealtime=yes. The
inactive example at
/usr/share/uperf-linux/systemd/50-realtime-opt-in.conf must be reviewed and
copied into /etc/systemd/system/uperf-linux.service.d/ explicitly, in addition
to enabling scheduler.realtime in the validated policy.

The package removal script stops the daemon and refuses to continue when
/run/uperf-linux/recovery.json remains. Do not delete that file manually:
it is the evidence needed to restore mutations made during the current boot.
